WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.392
Three nodes must agree: approve this payment, or decline it.

2
00:00:06.392 --> 00:00:09.284
The leader sends its verdict to both followers.

3
00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:13.996
Each follower passes on what the leader said, then decides by majority.

4
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.652
Now let one node lie.

5
00:00:15.652 --> 00:00:19.110
In world A, the leader is honest and says approve.

6
00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.329
Follower 2 lies: it tells follower 1 the leader said decline.

7
00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:25.443
In world B, the leader lies.

8
00:00:25.443 --> 00:00:32.077
It tells follower 1 approve and follower 2 decline, and follower 2 honestly passes decline on.

9
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:39.252
Follower 1 hears exactly the same thing in both worlds: approve from the leader, decline from follower 2.

10
00:00:39.252 --> 00:00:41.057
It can't tell who is lying.

11
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:43.893
Trust the leader, and world B ends split.

12
00:00:43.893 --> 00:00:47.462
Trust the peer, and world A overrules an honest leader.

13
00:00:47.462 --> 00:00:49.019
No rule gets both right.

14
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:50.307
Add a fourth node.

15
00:00:50.307 --> 00:00:56.199
Now each follower hears three verdicts: one from the leader, and two passed on by the others.

16
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.702
Let follower 2 lie.

17
00:00:57.702 --> 00:01:00.099
It spoils one verdict out of three.

18
00:01:00.099 --> 00:01:04.484
Followers 1 and 3 still hear two approves, so the majority wins.

19
00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:10.440
If the leader lies instead, it can split its story, but the followers pass on what they got.

20
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.353
Each hears approve twice and decline once, so all three agree.

21
00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:18.896
In general, surviving f lying nodes takes 3f + 1 of them.

22
00:01:18.896 --> 00:01:24.637
Crashes need only 2f + 1, because a crashed node never says anything false.

23
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:27.051
Most databases don't pay for that.

24
00:01:27.051 --> 00:01:33.444
Their replicas all run the same code, so a bug that makes one lie makes them all lie the same way.

25
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.708
So they assume nodes crash, and guard against the small lies: a bit flipped on disk or in memory.

26
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:44.570
A checksum on every record, checked on every read, catches those.

27
00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:49.599
One flipped bit turns 40 euros into 50, and the CRC no longer matches.

28
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.251
One liar takes four nodes to out-vote.

29
00:01:52.251 --> 00:01:57.024
Most systems assume crashes instead, and use checksums for the small lies.
